A||||3||mshill||Electrical tape for wiring harness?||||5||
Z||000000||mshill||03-01-2000||06:13 PM||||Ok, I am putting my V6 back together and wanted to clean up the wiring harness a bit and maybe dress it up some.  I found some red plastic looms at JC Whitney (on backorder of course) and some red electrical tape at Home Depot that says it is good to 105 celsius which is about 220F.  Is this going to be sufficient for most of the harness?<BR><p>[This message has been edited by mshill (edited 03-01-2000).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||terryk||03-01-2000||06:46 PM||||I've found that only the factory loom holds up well. I've used the 220F stuff and it's fine away from the engine, but tends to melt if it is close. <P>Playing around with the factory black loom it seems to handle about 300F.<P>I wish I could find the source for the factory loom. Maybe someone else has found a good supplier.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||03-01-2000||07:51 PM||||Those type of thing are about it.<P>You can also try <A HREF="http://www.eastwoodco.com" TARGET=_blank>Eastwood</A> who has the OEM plastic tape. ((This tape doesn't have adhesive that will goo up everything and collect crap.<P>Above 100C you will likely need silvered high temp tape to reflect the heat from the wire. Auto wire insulation is also not good much over 100C.<P>As long as the wires are away from the exhaust they should be fine.||||reg||1
Z||000003||stimpy||03-01-2000||07:51 PM||||I was able to go to my local O'Reilly's autoparts and get Taylor's wire loom delivered in about 24 hours by UPS. Got 50' of 1/4" and 10' of 3/4" in red- cost $8! Plenty to do the engine and still have some left over.  If you don't have O'Reilly's in your area, email me and I can hook you up.  If you want to see results, there is a pic of my engine in the general chat in the "engraved plenum-what up with that?" thread.<BR>||||reg||1
